<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="repeated-dosing-opening">
  <title>Repeated Dosing</title>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The model in Part 1 describes what happens after treatment stops. But
        before treatment stops, medication is usually taken on a schedule. A
        simple way to model repeated dosing is to update the concentration at
        regular time intervals.
      </p>

      <p>
        Suppose a dose is taken every <m>T</m> days. Between doses, the medication
        concentration decays exponentially. If the elimination constant is
        <m>k</m>, then after <m>T</m> days, the concentration is multiplied by
        <me>a = e^{-kT}.</me>
        Then a new dose increases the concentration by an amount <m>D</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>H_n</m> be the medication concentration immediately after the
        <m>n</m>th dose.
      </p>

      <p>
        Explain why the concentration immediately after the next dose satisfies
      </p>

      <me>H_{n+1} = aH_n + D.</me>

      <p>
        In this model, what does each term represent?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose the half-life is <m>8</m> days and a dose is taken every
        <m>7</m> days.
      </p>

      <p>
        First compute
      </p>

      <me>k = \frac{\ln 2}{8}</me>

      <p>
        and then compute
      </p>

      <me>a = e^{-7k}.</me>

      <p>
        Interpret the number <m>a</m> in words.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For simplicity, suppose <m>D=1</m> and <m>H_0=1</m>. Use the recurrence
      </p>

      <me>H_{n+1}=aH_n+1</me>

      <p>
        to compute <m>H_1,H_2,H_3,H_4</m>. Round your answers to three decimal
        places.
      </p>

      <tabular>
        <row>
          <cell><m>n</m></cell>
          <cell><m>H_n</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>0</m></cell>
          <cell><m>1</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>1</m></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>2</m></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>3</m></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>4</m></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A <em>steady state</em> for the recurrence
      </p>

      <me>H_{n+1}=aH_n+D</me>

      <p>
        is a value <m>H^\ast</m> such that if <m>H_n=H^\ast</m>, then
        <m>H_{n+1}=H^\ast</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Find a formula for <m>H^\ast</m> in terms of <m>a</m> and <m>D</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The recurrence
      </p>

      <me>H_{n+1}=aH_n+D</me>

      <p>
        is not a linear transformation because of the added constant <m>D</m>.
        However, it can be written as a matrix update by adding an extra
        coordinate:
      </p>

      <me>
        \begin{pmatrix}
        H_{n+1}\\
        1
        \end{pmatrix}
        =
        \begin{pmatrix}
        a &amp; D\\
        0 &amp; 1
        \end{pmatrix}
        \begin{pmatrix}
        H_n\\
        1
        \end{pmatrix}.
      </me>

      <p>
        Verify that this matrix equation gives the same recurrence.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="sage-interactive-repeated-dosing">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Use the Sage cell below to explore the repeated dosing model
      <m>H_{n+1}=aH_n+D</m>. The horizontal axis counts doses or dosing
      intervals. 
      This is a simplified model that does not include the continuous decay between doses, 
      but it captures the overall pattern of how concentration changes with repeated dosing.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
@interact
def repeated_dosing_model(
    a=slider(0.05, 0.95, 0.05, default=0.55, label="remaining fraction a"),
    D=slider(0.0, 5.0, 0.25, default=1.0, label="dose amount D"),
    H0=slider(0.0, 10.0, 0.25, default=1.0, label="initial concentration H0"),
    nmax=slider(1, 40, 1, default=20, label="number of dosing intervals")
):
    values = [H0]
    for n in range(nmax):
        values.append(a*values[-1] + D)

    pts = [(n, values[n]) for n in range(nmax+1)]

    if a != 1:
        Hstar = D/(1-a)
    else:
        Hstar = None

    p = list_plot(
        pts,
        plotjoined=True,
        marker='o',
        legend_label="H_n",
        thickness=2
    )

    if Hstar is not None:
        p += plot(
            Hstar,
            (x, 0, nmax),
            linestyle='--',
            thickness=2,
            legend_label="steady state H*"
        )

    show(p, axes_labels=["n", "H_n"])

    print("Recurrence:")
    print("H_(n+1) = a H_n + D")
    print("")
    print("a =", numerical_approx(a, digits=4))
    print("D =", numerical_approx(D, digits=4))
    print("H0 =", numerical_approx(H0, digits=4))

    if Hstar is not None:
        print("Steady state H* =", numerical_approx(Hstar, digits=4))
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      Try changing <m>a</m>. What happens when <m>a</m> is close to <m>0</m>?
      What happens when <m>a</m> is close to <m>1</m>?
    </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="sage-interactive-repeated-dosing-continuous">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Use the Sage cell below to explore the repeated dosing model with
      exponential decay between doses. The curve decays continuously between
      dose times, then jumps upward when a new dose is taken.
    </p>

    <sage>
      <input>
@interact
def repeated_dosing_continuous(
    k=slider(0.01, 0.5, 0.01, default=0.09, label="decay rate k"),
    T=slider(1, 21, 1, default=7, label="days between doses T"),
    D=slider(0.0, 5.0, 0.25, default=1.0, label="dose amount D"),
    H0=slider(0.0, 10.0, 0.25, default=1.0, label="initial concentration H0"),
    nmax=slider(1, 20, 1, default=8, label="number of doses")
):
    t = var('t')

    a = exp(-k*T)

    H_after = [H0]
    for n in range(nmax):
        H_after.append(a*H_after[-1] + D)

    p = Graphics()

    for n in range(nmax):
        start = n*T
        end = (n+1)*T
        Hn = H_after[n]
        curve = Hn*exp(-k*(t-start))
        p += plot(curve, (t, start, end), thickness=2)

        before_next = Hn*exp(-k*T)
        p += point((end, before_next), size=25)
        p += point((end, H_after[n+1]), size=25)

    dose_points = [(n*T, H_after[n]) for n in range(nmax+1)]
    p += list_plot(dose_points, marker='o', legend_label="after each dose")

    Hstar = D/(1-a)

    p += plot(Hstar, (t, 0, nmax*T), linestyle='--',
              thickness=2, legend_label="steady state after-dose level")

    show(p, axes_labels=["time t", "H(t)"])

    print("Between doses:")
    print("H(t) = H_n e^(-k(t-nT)) for nT &lt;= t \le (n+1)T")
    print("")
    print("Discrete update:")
    print("H_(n+1) = a H_n + D")
    print("a = e^(-kT) =", numerical_approx(a, digits=4))
    print("")
    print("Steady after-dose level:")
    print("H* =", numerical_approx(Hstar, digits=4))
      </input>
    </sage>

    <p>
      Try increasing <m>k</m>. What changes about the decay between doses?
      Try increasing <m>T</m>. What changes about the gap between the peak just
      after a dose and the trough just before the next dose?
    </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In this model, regular dosing does not simply keep the concentration
        constant. Instead, the concentration moves toward a steady pattern
        determined by the dose size, dosing interval, and half-life.
      </p>

      <p>
        Explain why this matters when thinking about interruptions to medication
        access. What information would be missing if we only knew whether someone
        had taken a dose recently?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>